import type { SkillTemplate, CommandTemplate } from '../types.js';

export function getOnboardSkillTemplate(): SkillTemplate {
  return {
    name: 'orbit-onboard',
    description: 'Single-pass onboarding reference for orbit - orientation to the self-contained tool, the canonical workflow, and the full command table. No demo changes, no interactive walkthrough.',
    instructions: getOnboardInstructions(),
    license: 'MIT',
    compatibility: 'Requires orbit CLI.',
    metadata: { author: 'openspec', version: '1.0' },
  };
}

function getOnboardInstructions(): string {
  return `
> **Note on \`/orbit:onboard\` emit semantics**: this command does NOT emit a run-summary JSON when invoked (composes with \`orbit-run-summary-emit\` \`Emit scope\`, which lists \`/orbit:onboard\` as a non-emit command). \`/orbit:onboard\` is a single-pass reference read — no workflow state advances, no artifacts produced in user project. Future orbit-additions or refactors MUST preserve this non-emission discipline.

This skill is the orbit-authored onboarding read for the \`/orbit:onboard\` slash command. Single-pass; renders Sections 1-5 sequentially.

This skill is the single source of the onboarding body; the \`/orbit:onboard\` command is a thin launcher that invokes it.

---

## Section 1: Orientation

You're reading this because orbit is installed and you ran \`/orbit:onboard\`. There's nothing to verify and nothing to "overlay": orbit-spec is a **self-contained** spec-driven workflow tool — a hard fork of openspec@1.3.1 with orbit's editorial layer built into the \`orbit\` CLI itself.

Quick orientation:

- **CLI**: \`orbit\` (run \`orbit --version\` to confirm it's on your PATH; the pinned engine version is 1.3.1).
- **Workspace**: \`orbit/\` holds your specs (\`orbit/specs/\`) and in-flight changes (\`orbit/changes/\`). If \`orbit/\` doesn't exist yet, run \`orbit init\` first.
- **Surface**: the \`/orbit:*\` slash commands and their skills are generated by \`orbit init\` — this onboarding read is one of them.

No install verification needed. On to what orbit is and how its workflow flows.

---

## Section 2: Identity statement

Orbit (orbit-spec) is a spec-driven workflow tool — a **self-contained hard fork** of \`@fission-ai/openspec@1.3.1\`. The upstream engine and orbit's editorial/judgment layer live together in one tool, invoked as \`orbit\`; there is no overlay and no separately-installed CLI. The engine is pinned at 1.3.1 (deliberate, surgical upgrades only). Orbit shares concepts with upstream OpenSpec but ships an opinionated workflow with distinct disciplines, not a delta on top of upstream's defaults.

What makes orbit distinctive vs running upstream alone:

- **Editorial review** — \`/orbit:review\` runs a 9-pass editorial pass over the artifacts (proposal mode) or a 7-pass pass over the post-apply product state (system mode, which wraps upstream's \`verify-change\` as Pass 0). \`/orbit:review-external\` packages a self-contained prompt for a different AI (codex / fresh Claude / GPT) to do a second-opinion pass; the external AI commits findings back to the repo. \`/orbit:address-reviews\` walks each finding through a structured pushback → classify → fix → ripple-cascade → remove lifecycle (works on both inline \`@review:\` markers and external-review findings files). Walk-mode (per-finding) is the default; \`--batch\` opts in. Cascade-by-default auto-applies ripple edits to IN-set files (everything outside the four lifecycle-invariant OUT categories); \`--no-cascade\` opts out.

- **Drift audit** — \`/orbit:audit-drift\` scans for drift between captured knowledge (specs, lenses, governing docs) and reality across four categories: vocabulary residue (FROM names of renamed requirements still appearing), lens staleness (perspectives or critical-paths referencing nonexistent capabilities), cross-doc consistency (CLAUDE.md / project.md disagreement with current specs), archive coherence (ADDED requirements missing from baseline, RENAMED FROM names lingering). Standalone for "something feels off" checks; auto-invoked as system-mode review Pass 6 and as a pre-archive sweep.

- **Capture (lenses)** — \`orbit/lenses/perspectives.md\` records named callers worth simulating from during review; \`orbit/lenses/critical-paths.md\` records flows worth walking end-to-end. Lenses grow organically through \`/orbit:explore\` capture triggers; system-mode review Passes 4-5 consult them.

- **Program architecture** — \`/orbit:architect\` and \`orbit architecture status\` (D-arc Stage D2): the cross-change declaration tier above per-change flow. Architecture docs under \`orbit/architecture/\` declare program stages via parseable heading conventions (\`### Stage <id> — <title>\` + optional \`Depends-on:\`/\`Complete:\` slots); stage state is COMPUTED from disk (which changes/explores cite the stage via \`architecture: {doc, stage}\` metadata), never stored. \`/orbit:review --as architecture\` reviews a doc (6 passes, no gate). Never a required entry point — \`/orbit:explore\` plus hand-editing remains first-class.

- **JSON run-summary emission** — every editorial and lifecycle command emits a JSON run-summary at command completion (or per chunk for \`/orbit:apply\`). Summaries persist to \`orbit/changes/<name>/.orbit-runs/\` (per-change) or \`orbit/.orbit-runs/\` (project-wide). The schema follows a universal spine with per-command extensions. (Note: \`/orbit:onboard\` is one of the few commands that does NOT emit — it's a reference read, not a workflow advancement.)

- **Four execution disciplines** codified in \`orbit-conventions\`:
  - **Read-before-reference** (authoring-time): when generating code, specs, or docs that name a specific construct, read the actual definition first. Don't infer the shape from common patterns or training-data conventions.
  - **Change completeness** (modification-time): substantive modifications to a change-in-flight must apply fully across ALL affected artifacts before being declared done. Known residue MUST NOT be left for a downstream review to catch.
  - **Pushback** (review-time): verify each flagged issue against current state before acting. Stale findings get reported with evidence and suppressed.
  - **No-chaining** (turn boundary): the AI invokes at most the orbit workflow commands the user's message explicitly requests — one command per explicit request; it recommends, never invokes. Completing a command ends the turn at the position readout and the engine's eligibility line (\`Ready when you are:\`); gate \`passed\` means *eligible*, not *commanded*.

---

## Section 3: Canonical-flow walkthrough

Orbit's canonical flow has 9 phases. Each maps to a \`/orbit:*\` slash command. The flow is:

\`\`\`
   explore ─→ propose ─→ review ─→ address-reviews ─→ apply ─→ verify ─→
   review --as system ─→ address-reviews ─→ archive
\`\`\`

**Phase 1 — explore (\`/orbit:explore [<name>]\`)**: Thinking-mode entry. The user explores an idea, problem, or comparison; orbit acts as thinking partner. In named mode (\`/orbit:explore <name>\`), decisions auto-capture to \`orbit/explore/<name>/explore.md\` in a 5-section convention (Premise / Decisions / Open questions / Considered & out / References). In bare mode (no name), the explore stays in conversation until 2+ substantive decisions emerge, then prompts to crystallize a name. Capture triggers also offer to save **lenses** — \`perspectives.md\` records named callers worth validating from during review (e.g., "API client", "Claude Desktop using MCP"); \`critical-paths.md\` records flows worth walking end-to-end (e.g., "First-time user adds their first device"). Lenses grow organically; system-mode review Passes 4-5 consult them.

**Phase 2 — propose (\`/orbit:propose <name>\`)**: Generates the change artifacts (proposal, design, specs, tasks) from the explore.md (consume mode) or from a prompted description (standalone mode). Consume mode treats \`explore.md\` as authoritative seed: Premise → proposal motivation; Decisions → spec deltas + design + tasks; Considered & out → design's "Alternatives considered"; References → contextual reads. Open questions become per-question prompts during propose with three resolution paths (resolve now → Decision; defer → \`@review:\` marker in artifacts; abandon → moves to Considered & out). After artifact generation, the staging directory at \`orbit/explore/<name>/\` moves to \`orbit/changes/<name>/\` (the historical \`explore.md\` persists alongside the new artifacts). Emits a completion run summary to \`orbit/changes/<name>/.orbit-runs/propose-<TS>.json\` ("T0" is reserved for review-external's prompt-packaging emit).

**Phase 3 — review (proposal mode, \`/orbit:review <name> --as proposal\`)**: Editorial pre-apply pass over the artifacts. 9 passes: Structure & Delta Integrity (validate runs clean, deltas use ADDED/MODIFIED/REMOVED/RENAMED correctly) / Internal Coherence (proposal aligns with design aligns with specs aligns with tasks) / Cross-Doc Coherence (CLAUDE.md and project.md remain accurate after this change) / Archive Consistency (ADDED don't contradict baseline, RENAMED FROM names exist) / Codegen Readiness (no implicit requirements, no ambiguity) / Gap Hunt (could a fresh AI implement this from these specs alone?) / Drift Hunt (old vocabulary lingering) / Inline Review Marker Residue (\`@review:\` markers must be 0) / Pre-Handoff Sweep. Findings roll into a 3-dimension scorecard (Completeness / Correctness / Coherence) with CRITICAL / WARNING / SUGGESTION severities. The final-assessment line is single-voiced and engine-sourced (C1): it renders the computed \`review-proposal\` gate state plus one canonical next command ("All checks passed. gate review-proposal PASSED. Ready when you are: /orbit:apply <name> — user-invoked." vs "X critical issue(s) found. Fix before /orbit:apply." vs, when the cadence's independent slot is uncovered, "... gate review-proposal UNPASSED — the cadence's independent slot is uncovered. Ready when you are: /orbit:review --as proposal --fresh <name>."). Run summary persists to \`orbit/changes/<name>/.orbit-runs/review-proposal-<TS>.json\`.

**Phase 4 — address-reviews (\`/orbit:address-reviews <name>\` or \`--from-file <path>\`)**: Walks each finding (or each \`@review:\` marker) through a structured lifecycle: pushback → classify → fix → ripple-cascade → remove-marker. Walk-mode (per-finding) is the default; \`--batch\` opts in. Pushback verifies against current state (a finding cited at file:line might already be resolved; stale findings get suppressed with evidence). Classification routes to one of four outcomes: stale (already resolved; just remove marker), trivial fix (apply directly), decision required (surface 2-4 options via \`AskUserQuestion\` — or a structured A/B/discuss fork when the recommendation is disjunctive), unresolvable (file as task or convert to \`@todo:\` / \`@review(escalated):\`). Ripple cascade auto-applies parallel edits to IN-set files (everything outside the four lifecycle-invariant OUT categories: \`.orbit-runs/\`, baseline \`orbit/specs/\`, cross-change/archive dirs, safe-exclusions); \`--no-cascade\` opts out. Marker removal is invariant on resolution (unless \`--keep-resolved-markers\` is set). Emits a resolution-log run summary. **Cross-AI loop**: \`/orbit:review-external <name>\` packages a self-contained prompt at \`orbit/changes/<name>/.orbit-runs/external-prompt-<as>-<TS>.md\` — paste into a different AI session, the external AI commits findings back to a sibling file, then \`/orbit:address-reviews <name> --from-file <findings-path>\` ingests and walks each. See the \`orbit-review-external\` skill for the full external-review loop (recommended-session logic, output format, iteration tracking).

**Phase 5 — apply (\`/orbit:apply <name>\`)**: Implements tasks from \`tasks.md\` chunk by chunk. Chunks are natural pause points — at each chunk's end, the SKILL emits a run summary to \`.orbit-runs/apply-<TS>.json\` describing what was changed, what's done, what remains, and the next recommended action. The user can interrupt between chunks. Apply pauses if a task surfaces a premise problem (e.g., implementation reveals a design issue) and prompts for direction — \`@review(escalated):\` markers, artifact updates, or scope cuts are all valid responses.

**Phase 6 — verify (\`/orbit:verify <name>\`)**: Runs upstream's \`orbit-verify-change\` skill — structural correctness check confirming tasks are complete, spec coverage holds, and design adherence is intact. orbit composes this as the structural gate before system-mode review.

**Phase 7 — review (system mode, \`/orbit:review <name> --as system\`)**: Post-apply review of the whole product state. Pass 0 wraps \`orbit-verify-change\` (delegated structural check). Passes 1-6: Baseline Compliance (does this change break archived \`orbit/specs/\` requirements?) / Cohesion (callers/dependents outside the tasks list — signature drift, ripple effects) / Surface Walk (every CLI/MCP/HTTP/public-function surface in \`orbit/specs/\` still coherent?) / Perspective Reviews (for each named perspective in \`orbit/lenses/perspectives.md\`, simulate the caller's POV) / Critical-Path Scan (for each flow in \`orbit/lenses/critical-paths.md\`, walk end-to-end) / Drift / Residue (invokes \`/orbit:audit-drift\` as a library function). Run summary persists to \`.orbit-runs/review-system-<TS>.json\`.

**Phase 8 — address-reviews (system-mode findings)**: Same \`/orbit:address-reviews\` lifecycle as Phase 4, applied to system-mode review's findings. The cross-AI external loop also works here (\`/orbit:review-external --as system\`).

**Phase 9 — archive (\`/orbit:archive <name>\`)**: Moves the change to \`orbit/changes/archive/<YYYY-MM-DD>-<name>/\` and runs sync-specs (invokes the \`orbit-sync-specs\` upstream-required primitive) to propagate spec deltas (ADDED/MODIFIED/REMOVED/RENAMED) into baseline at \`orbit/specs/\`. Pre-archive sweeps: unresolved \`@review:\` marker check (prompts to address or convert to \`@todo:\`); audit-drift sweep (runs \`/orbit:audit-drift --context pre-archive\`). The per-change \`.orbit-runs/\` directory travels with the change into the archive, preserving the full audit trail. Run summary emits to \`.orbit-runs/archive-<TS>.json\` inside the archived location.

---

## Section 4: Quick-reference command table

The \`/orbit:*\` command surface. Every row is generated into your project by \`orbit init\`. One-line descriptions follow.

| Command | One-line description |
|---------|---------------------|
| \`/orbit:address-reviews [<scope>] [--from-file <path>] [--batch] [--no-cascade]\` | Walk inline \`@review:\` markers or \`--from-file\` findings (external-review markdown, internal review JSON, or audit-drift JSON — auto-detected by content sniff) through pushback → classify → fix → ripple-cascade → remove. Walk-mode per-finding by default (\`--batch\` opts in); cascade auto-applies to IN-set files by default (\`--no-cascade\` opts out; OUT-set = audit-trail, baseline specs, cross-change/archive, safe-exclusions). Resolves rather than scans. Auto-discovers internal review/audit-drift JSON when invoked with a change name and no markers found. Disjunctive recommendations surface as structured A/B/discuss forks. |
| \`/orbit:apply <name>\` | Implement tasks from \`tasks.md\` chunk-by-chunk with end-of-chunk run-summary emits. Pauses on premise problems. |
| \`/orbit:architect [<doc-name>]\` | Author or resume an architecture doc (program layer): parseable stage declarations, ruling capture, engine-rendered state, completion-ratification flow. Never a required entry point. |
| \`/orbit:archive <name>\` | Move change to \`orbit/changes/archive/\`, run sync-specs to propagate deltas to baseline. Pre-archive \`@review:\` marker check + audit-drift sweep. |
| \`/orbit:audit-drift [<change-name>]\` | Scan for drift between captured knowledge and reality (4 categories: vocabulary, lens, cross-doc, archive). Standalone or library-call. |
| \`/orbit:bulk-archive\` | Archive multiple completed changes at once. |
| \`/orbit:continue <name>\` | Create the next artifact in the change's workflow. |
| \`/orbit:explore [<name>]\` | Thinking-mode entry. Named: auto-captures decisions to \`explore.md\`. Bare: stays in chat until crystallization. |
| \`/orbit:ff\` | Fast-forward through artifact creation in one go. |
| \`/orbit:new <name>\` | Start a new change using the experimental artifact workflow. |
| \`/orbit:onboard\` | Run this skill (the reference walkthrough you're reading now). |
| \`/orbit:propose <name>\` | Generate proposal + design + specs + tasks artifacts in one step. Consume mode reads \`orbit/explore/<name>/explore.md\` if present. |
| \`/orbit:review <name> [--as proposal\\|system\\|architecture] [--fresh]\` | Editorial review: 9 passes proposal-mode (pre-apply), 7 passes system-mode (post-apply, wraps \`verify-change\` as Pass 0), or 6 passes architecture-mode (a doc, no gate). |
| \`/orbit:review-external <name> [--as proposal\\|system]\` | Package a self-contained prompt for a different AI to do a second-opinion review. Commits prompt to repo; external AI commits findings back. |
| \`/orbit:sync <name>\` | Sync delta specs from a change to main specs without archiving. |
| \`/orbit:verify <name>\` | Run upstream's structural verify-change (tasks done, spec coverage, design adherence). Composed as Pass 0 of system-mode review. |

This table documents the user-invocable command surface.

---

## Section 5: Try-it nudge

Two paths from here, depending on what you have:

- **If you have a concrete project idea** — run \`/orbit:explore <your-change-name>\`. Replace \`<your-change-name>\` with a kebab-case name describing what you want to build or change (e.g., \`add-user-auth\`, \`migrate-postgres-to-sqlite\`, \`redesign-archive-flow\`). Named-mode explore starts a thinking partnership: orbit asks clarifying questions, surfaces threads, captures decisions to \`orbit/explore/<name>/explore.md\` as they emerge. When decisions stabilize, \`/orbit:propose <your-change-name>\` generates proposal + design + specs + tasks from the explore.md.

- **If you're orienting and don't have a concrete idea yet** — run \`/orbit:explore\` (no name). Bare-mode explore stays in thinking-mode without committing to a change. Orbit acts as thinking partner; if 2+ substantive decisions emerge during the conversation, orbit prompts you to crystallize a name. Useful for investigating a vague concern, comparing approaches, or just orienting yourself to the codebase.

When you've gathered findings worth acting on, see \`/orbit:address-reviews\` to walk them. When you want a project-wide drift check, see \`/orbit:audit-drift\`. When you want a cross-AI second opinion, see \`/orbit:review-external\`.`;
}

export function getOrbitOnboardCommandTemplate(): CommandTemplate {
  return {
    name: 'ORBIT: Onboard',
    description: 'Single-pass onboarding reference - orient to orbit, its canonical workflow, and the command surface',
    category: 'Workflow',
    tags: ['workflow', 'onboarding', 'reference', 'learning'],
    content: `Single-pass onboarding reference for orbit.

This command is a thin launcher: invoke the \`orbit-onboard\` skill (e.g., \`/orbit:onboard\`). The skill carries the complete onboarding body. Do not improvise the walkthrough from this file; load the skill.`,
  };
}
